  "image_name": "d19b66c21b456d87f5ff292850b0411b.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes templated routes for making complex porous or hollow particles and how their composition and internal architecture can be tuned. It contrasts two main interfacial strategies: solid–liquid and liquid–liquid templating. In both cases, surfactants and reactants gather at an interface, where nucleation of precursors occurs and different molecular species organize into solid regions. This interfacial assembly then leads to structured particles with controlled shell formation.\n\nAcross the figure, the key message is that templating can produce a wide range of morphologies and functions, including core–shell or yolk–shell particles, multiple concentric shells, and anisotropic structures with hydrophilic or hydrophobic character. The chemistry used to build the shell can vary as well, with examples based on silica, carbon, and metal oxides, and the surface can be modified by coatings or polymer-like interlayers to create nucleation sites.\n\nThe lower part emphasizes hollow-structure diversity after component incorporation. Different loading or deposition approaches appear to generate catalyst-containing hollow particles, streamlined asymmetric cavities, and multi-chamber structures with embedded metal nanoparticles. A final schematic suggests these architectures can be further reshaped through dissolution, repolymerization, self-assembly, and chemical tailoring, linking molecular building blocks to hierarchically assembled porous materials.",
